    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "Italy's ADM launches a central online gambling complaints portal on 10 September with 72-hour response rule",
    "meta": "SBC News | 7 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "ADM announced on 3 August that it will launch the \"Portale delle segnalazioni\", a central complaints portal for online gambling disputes, on 10 September as part of the sector's reorganisation. Operators will have 72 working hours to respond to a customer complaint, excluding public holidays, and customers must register via SPID or Italy's electronic ID card, supplying player account and operator details. ADM will not intervene in the first instance but can open a supplementary investigation where an operator fails to respond or answers unsatisfactorily, with a further 72-hour information deadline.",
    "why": "A state-run complaints channel with a hard clock turns customer-service SLAs into a regulatory obligation, and puts the quality of an operator's dispute records — transaction history, bonus terms, limit changes, KYC decisions — directly in front of the regulator. Platform teams should expect to be asked for evidence packs, not narratives.",
    "expanded": "Once ADM issues its final response the complaint is closed and cannot be resubmitted, which raises the stakes on getting the first reply right. ADM will also keep working with state IT and cybersecurity group SOGEI on a \"protective shield\" against unlicensed operators, partly funded by the new online licensing regime under which 48 operators secured concessions at €7m each — a reminder that Italy's newly-paid-up licensees now have a direct commercial interest in enforcement working.",
