    "why": "An EU-level gambling levy would be the first serious attempt to tax the sector above the member-state layer, and the S&D group frames it explicitly as a step toward harmonising fragmented European gambling law. For anyone with a Malta-centred operating model, that combination of tax and harmonisation is the structural risk worth tracking, however unlikely the levy is to survive negotiation.",
    "expanded": "The Commission wants more emphasis on EU \"own resources\", estimating that proposed new streams — e-waste, tobacco excise, contributions from large Single Market companies — could raise around €58.5bn a year in 2025 prices, against a budget that includes €600bn of COVID-debt repayment and €150bn in structural and military aid for Ukraine. Abela's objection is that a levy would disproportionately hit Malta's MGA-licensed economy, and Cyprus, which handed the presidency to Ireland, is in a comparable position. Ireland itself only opened GRAI applications for Remote Betting and Remote Betting Intermediary Licences in February.",
