    "summary": "Liam Normanton, Head of Sports at RightBet, told SBC News its World Cup football book ran a noticeably higher margin than its regular domestic book but with \"genuinely violent day-to-day swings\", moving from a significant single-day loss to a significant single-day gain within 48 hours during the knockout stage. Correct score and both-teams-to-score were the clearest underperformers alongside handicap and match bet/total combinations, while vanilla match odds stayed stable and half-time/full-time performed well. Anytime goalscorer was the standout by volume and held up on margin.",
    "why": "Very few operators publish a candid market-by-market breakdown of where a major tournament actually hurt them, and the pattern — precise-scoreline products breaking while simple outcome markets held — is directly actionable for anyone setting limits and cash-out rules for the next big event.",
    "expanded": "On the day Belgium's comeback against Senegal settled, correct score, BTTS, next goalscorer and over/under all landed negative together, which is the correlated-exposure problem in miniature. Mid-tournament the trading team tightened limits and repriced correct score and BTTS more conservatively, and Normanton's three stated priorities are to act on scoreline-market repricing earlier — the pattern was visible in week one — invest in reaction speed for player markets, and build cleaner measurement discipline, since the World Cup tail overlapped RightBet's racing launch and made behavioural shifts hard to attribute.",
    "source": "https://sbcnews.co.uk/features/2026/08/10/rightbet-world-cup-lessons/",
