    "meta": "The Event Horizon | 6 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Dustin Gouker opens with the thesis \"If Kalshi has learned one thing, it's that velocity and volatility mean more trading\", using it to explain why sports and combos remain 70-80% of Kalshi notional volume even in a slow sports period, and why crypto has climbed from under 5% of volume earlier this year to more than 20% on a given day. He cites Ticker Tracker data showing $1.5bn traded on crypto markets over a trailing seven days, roughly $1.1bn of it on Bitcoin 15-minute markets, with seven of the top ten crypto markets being 15-minute contracts. Kalshi has since extended the format to 15-minute gold and silver markets alongside hourly temperature markets.",
    "why": "This reframes prediction-market growth as a product-design template rather than a content story: compress the resolution window and volume follows, regardless of what the underlying market is about. That is a mechanic any operator can reason about, and one that regulators are likely to notice.",
    "expanded": "Gouker points to Barron's reporting that \"assets with 15-minute markets could eventually include equities, such as Apple stock\", and concludes: \"I'll bet that we see way more of this integrated into US prediction markets moving forward. The only limit on creating short-resolution markets might be the companies' imagination.\" He sets this against Flutter's Q2, where FanDuel conceded H1 prediction-market progress was \"slower than planned\" and Jackson argued exchanges historically take small share against sportsbooks because market makers cannot offer generosity.",
    "commentary": "The argument is that short-resolution contracts are gamification by another name — Gouker writes explicitly that \"we are still in the early stages of gamifying prediction markets\", noting Polymarket International already runs 5-minute crypto markets that US venues have yet to match. The uncomfortable implication is that the fastest-growing segment of a CFTC-regulated market looks structurally closer to high-frequency slot play than to hedging.",
    "takeaway": "Watch this as a two-sided signal. Commercially, short-cycle event contracts are a genuine engagement mechanic worth understanding before deciding whether to build or partner. From a safer-gambling and regulatory-design standpoint, it is also precisely the pattern Brazil's SPA is moving to ban in casino — speed, repetition and compressed feedback loops — which makes a future collision between prediction-market product design and player-protection rules fairly likely.",
    "source": "https://nexteventhorizon.substack.com/p/the-15-minute-ification-of-prediction-markets",
