    "summary": "Old Mutual's Savings and Investment Monitor 2026 found 53% of employed South Africans aged 18-65 earning at least R8,000 a month gamble, and 42% say they gamble frequently hoping to cover living costs or reduce debt. Reliance is highest among lower earners: 52% of those earning R8,000-R15,000 a month use gambling to supplement cash flow, and nearly six in ten adults aged 18-29 gamble against 40% of over-50s. Reported financial difficulty caused by gambling rose to 22% in 2026 from 12% a year earlier, reaching 26% among those earning under R30,000 a month.",
    "why": "The finding that 42% gamble specifically to cover living costs or reduce debt is a different and more serious claim than high participation rates on their own — it describes gambling substituting for income, which is the pattern that tends to precede legislative intervention.",
    "expanded": "Sports betting is the most popular activity at 63% of gamblers, ahead of casino slots (46%), lottery (42%), table games (26%) and horse racing (11%), with about 9% gambling daily or almost daily, against NGB data putting 2025 South African turnover above R1.5trn. The doubling of self-reported gambling-driven financial difficulty in twelve months is the statistic most likely to be quoted in the National Gambling Amendment Bill debate, and it lands while the industry is already defending its advertising and affordability practices.",
